  "summary": "Yoruba resist-dyed cloth made with cassava paste resist patterns",
  "description": "Features intricate white patterns on deep indigo backgrounds, created using cassava starch applied through stencils or freehand. Developed as women's craft in Yoruba communities, with patterns often encoding proverbs and cultural knowledge.",
  "culturalContext": "Initially produced in south-western Nigeria, adire textiles traveled to northern Africa through Yoruba traders and trader families. Some families who chose to migrate up north began creating adire cloths to market to other women. Because it is usually made by women, patterns and themes of the Adire are passed down from mother to daughter within families. However, certain motifs can depend on the artist's abilities and craftmanship, as well as skills taught from older generations. The patterns of Adire are often representations of plants, animals, tools, and conceptual themes. Traditional themes are categorized into geometric, figural, skewmorphic, letters, and celestiomorphic types.",
